[Event "Sicilian: Najdorf"]

{The Najdorf (5...a6) is the most respected Sicilian. ...a6 stops Nb5 and Bb5, prepares ...e5 or ...e6 and a queenside expansion with ...b5. Black trades a wing pawn for White's d-pawn and gets the half-open c-file.}
1. e4 {White takes the centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... c5 {[%csl Gd4] The Sicilian: fights for d4 from the flank and creates an unbalanced game.}
2. Nf3 {Develops and prepares d4. The knight on f3 controls e5 and d4.}
2... d6 {Controls e5 and prepares ...Nf6 without allowing e5.}
3. d4 {Opens the centre. The pawn on d4 controls e5 and eyes the pawn on c5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
3... cxd4 {[%csl Gc8] Trades a wing pawn for a centre pawn. The c-file opens for Black.}
4. Nxd4 {Recaptures centrally. Recaptures on d4, restoring the material balance.}
4... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Hits e4, forcing Nc3 before White can play c4.}
5. Nc3 {Defends e4. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.}
5... a6 {[%csl Gb5] The Najdorf: covers b5 and prepares ...e5 or ...b5.}
6. Be3 {The English Attack: Be3, f3, Qd2, O-O-O and g4.}
 (6. Bg5 {The old main line, pinning the knight. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6.}
6... e6 {Solid: blunts the pin and keeps d5 covered. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5.}
7. f4 {Prepares e5 or f5. Supports the centre: the pawn on f4 controls e5 and supports the bishop on g5.}
7... Be7 {Breaks the pin. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8. Qf3 {Supports e4 and prepares O-O-O. The queen goes to f3 and supports the pawn on e4.}
8... Qc7 {Covers e5 and the c-file. The queen goes to c7.}
9. O-O-O {Opposite-side castling: a race begins. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
9... Nbd7 {Covers e5 and f6; ...b5 is next. Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.}
)
 (6. Be2 {The quiet Opocensky. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Kicks the knight and claims d4. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the knight on d4.}
7. Nb3 {Moves the knight out of attack to b3 and controls d4.}
7... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
8. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8... O-O {Castles; Black has a comfortable game. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
)
 (6. Bc4 {The Fischer–Sozin bishop. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... e6 {Blunts the bishop. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5.}
7. Bb3 {b5 tempos. Regroups the bishop to b3 and controls d5.}
7... b5 {Gains queenside space. The pawn on b5 takes a4 and c4 away from the opponent.}
8. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
)
6... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Kicks the knight and claims space, leaving a backward d6 pawn as the price.}
7. Nb3 {Moves the knight out of attack to b3 and controls d4.}
7... Be6 {[%csl Gd5] Controls d5, the key square. Develops the bishop to e6, where it controls d5. White's last move put a knight on b3, which this now pressures.}
8. f3 {Supports e4 and prepares g4. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls g4 and supports the pawn on e4.}
8... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
9. Qd2 {Connects for O-O-O. The queen goes to d2 and controls d4 and d5.}
9... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
10. O-O-O {Opposite castling. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
10... Nbd7 {Covers f6 and c5. Develops the knight to d7, where it supports the pawn on e5.} *

[Event "Sicilian: Dragon"]

{In the Dragon (5...g6), Black's bishop on g7 is the best piece in the position. White usually attacks on the kingside with the Yugoslav Attack.}
1. e4 {White claims the centre and opens lines for the queen and f1-bishop. The most popular first move.}
1... c5 {Sicilian. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
2. Nf3 {White develops and prepares d4 to open the Sicilian.}
2... d6 {Prepares ...Nf6. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls e5 and supports the pawn on c5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
3. d4 {Opens. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls e5 and eyes the pawn on c5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
3... cxd4 {Black trades the c-pawn for White's d-pawn: the Sicilian bargain. Black gets an extra central pawn and the half-open c-file.}
4. Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight.}
4... Nf6 {Hits e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
5. Nc3 {White defends e4 and develops. Now Black chooses the variation: ...a6 (Najdorf), ...g6 (Dragon), ...e6 or ...Nc6.}
5... g6 {[%cal Gg6g7] The Dragon: the bishop goes to g7. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
6. Be3 {The Yugoslav Attack. Develops the bishop to e3, where it supports the knight on d4.}
6... Bg7 {[%cal Gg7a1] The Dragon bishop rakes the long diagonal. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. f3 {Supports e4 and g4. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls g4 and supports the pawn on e4.}
7... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8. Qd2 {Prepares Bh6 and O-O-O. The queen goes to d2 and supports the knight on d4.}
8... Nc6 {Black develops the last minor piece and puts pressure on d4. White now chooses O-O-O or Bc4.}
9. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Stops ...d5. Develops the bishop to c4, where it controls d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 (9. O-O-O {Allows the central break. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
9... d5 {[%cal Gd5e4] The thematic break. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and eyes the pawn on e4.}
10. exd5 {White takes.}
10... Nxd5 {Black recaptures; the d5-knight is central. White's Nxc6 next opens the b-file for Black.}
11. Nxc6 {White takes on c6. Black must recapture with the b-pawn, opening the b-file toward White's king.}
11... bxc6 {Recaptures towards the centre. Recaptures on c6, restoring the material balance.}
12. Bd4 {Fights the Dragon bishop. The bishop goes to d4 and controls e5.}
)
9... Bd7 {Black develops and connects rooks. Plans: ...Rc8 and ...Ne5 to fight for c4.}
10. O-O-O {Castles long. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
10... Rc8 {[%cal Gc8c3] The c-file. The rook goes to c8.}
11. Bb3 {Removes the bishop from ...Ne5 threats. Regroups the bishop to b3 and controls d5.}
11... Ne5 {[%cal Ge5c4] Heads for c4. The knight goes to e5.} *

[Event "Sicilian: Sveshnikov"]

{The Sveshnikov (5...e5) gives up d5 and accepts a backward d-pawn for activity and the bishop pair.}
1. e4 {White claims the centre and opens lines for the queen and f1-bishop. The most popular first move.}
1... c5 {Sicilian. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
2. Nf3 {White develops and prepares d4 to open the Sicilian.}
2... Nc6 {Black develops and controls d4. Black keeps flexible between ...e5, ...g6 or ...e6.}
3. d4 {Opens. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls e5 and eyes the pawn on c5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
3... cxd4 {Black trades the c-pawn for White's d-pawn: the Sicilian bargain.}
4. Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight.}
4... Nf6 {Hits e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
5. Nc3 {White defends e4 and develops. Black's ...e5 next is the Sveshnikov.}
5... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] The Sveshnikov: kicks the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the knight on d4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
6. Ndb5 {Heads for d6 and d5. Moves the knight out of attack to b5 and controls d4.}
6... d6 {Covers d6. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
7. Bg5 {Pins the defender of d5. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6.}
7... a6 {Kicks the knight. The pawn on a6 eyes the knight on b5.}
8. Na3 {Moves the knight out of attack to a3.}
8... b5 {[%cal Gb5b4] Cuts off the a3 knight. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on b5 takes a4 and c4 away from the opponent.}
9. Bxf6 {Removes the defender. Takes the knight on f6; Black will want to recapture.}
 (9. Nd5 {Occupies d5. The knight goes to d5.}
9... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
10. Bxf6 {White gives up the bishop to gain control of d5. Black must recapture, damaging his kingside or losing the bishop pair.}
10... Bxf6 {Recaptures with the bishop pair. Recaptures on f6, restoring the material balance.}
11. c3 {Brings the a3 knight back. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
)
9... gxf6 {Recaptures with doubled pawns but the bishop pair.}
10. Nd5 {Occupies d5. The knight goes to d5. Black's last move put a pawn on f6, which this now pressures.}
10... f5 {[%cal Gf5e4] Hits e4 at once. Supports the centre: the pawn on f5 controls g4 and eyes the pawn on e4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.} *

[Event "Sicilian: Accelerated Dragon"]

{The Accelerated Dragon (4...g6) skips ...d6 to keep ...d5 in one move.}
1. e4 {White claims the centre and opens lines for the queen and f1-bishop. The most popular first move.}
1... c5 {Sicilian. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
2. Nf3 {White develops and prepares d4 to open the Sicilian.}
2... Nc6 {Black develops and controls d4. Black keeps flexible between ...e5, ...g6 or ...e6.}
3. d4 {Opens. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls e5 and eyes the pawn on c5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
3... cxd4 {Black trades the c-pawn for White's d-pawn: the Sicilian bargain.}
4. Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight. Black's ...g6 next is the Accelerated Dragon.}
4... g6 {The Accelerated Dragon. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
5. c4 {The Maroczy Bind. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
 (5. Nc3 {White develops and supports e4. Black's ...Bg7 next completes the fianchetto.}
5... Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. The g7-bishop points at d4 and the long diagonal: that's the Dragon's main weapon.}
6. Be3 {White develops and supports d4. Black's ...Nf6 next.}
6... Nf6 {The knight develops with pressure on e4 and Black is ready to castle. Typical mistake: delaying this for pawn moves and getting caught in the centre.}
7. Bc4 {Aims at f7. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7... Qa5 {Pins the c3 knight. The queen goes to a5 and controls d5 and e5.}
8. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
9. Bb3 {Regroups the bishop to b3 and controls d5.}
9... d6 {Black supports ...e5 and opens the c8-bishop. White's plan: f3 or h3.}
)
5... Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. The g7-bishop points at d4 and the long diagonal.}
6. Be3 {White develops and supports d4. Black's ...Nf6 next.}
6... Nf6 {The knight develops with pressure on e4 and Black is ready to castle. Typical mistake: delaying this for pawn moves and getting caught in the centre.}
7. Nc3 {White develops and supports e4.}
7... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8. Be2 {Quiet and solid. The bishop backs up the f3-knight (no annoying ...Bg4 pin pressure) and White is one move from castling.}
8... d6 {Black supports ...e5 and opens the c8-bishop.}
9. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
9... Bd7 {Black develops and connects rooks. Plans: ...a6 and ...b5 or ...Qa5.} *

[Event "Sicilian: Anti-Sicilians"]

{2.c3 and 3.Bb5+.}
1. e4 {White claims the centre and opens lines for the queen and f1-bishop.}
1... c5 {Sicilian. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
2. c3 {The Alapin. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
 (2. Nf3 {White develops and prepares d4 or Bb5.}
2... d6 {Najdorf move order. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls e5 and supports the pawn on c5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
3. Bb5+ {The Moscow. It is check, so the reply is forced.}
3... Bd7 {Blocks. Develops the bishop to d7, where it controls e6 and f5. It attacks the bishop on b5, which is undefended.}
4. Bxd7+ {Trades. It is check, so the reply is forced. It gives check.}
4... Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen. Black's queen is active but can be hit by Nc3 or c4.}
5. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5... Nc6 {Black develops and controls d4.}
6. c3 {Prepares d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
6... Nf6 {Black develops and pressures e4. White's Re1 or Qe2 defends it.}
7. Re1 {Supports e4. The rook goes to e1 and supports the pawn on e4.}
)
2... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Hits e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3. e5 {Kicks the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d6 and eyes the knight on f6.}
3... Nd5 {Central knight. Moves the knight out of attack to d5.}
4. d4 {White builds a big centre. Black's ...cxd4 next opens lines.}
4... cxd4 {Black takes the pawn. White recaptures with cxd4 or Nf3.}
5. Nf3 {White develops toward e5. The d4-pawn is not yet recaptured.}
5... Nc6 {Black develops and pressures e5 and d4. White's cxd4 next.}
6. cxd4 {White recaptures and rebuilds the centre. Black's plan: ...d6 or ...e6.}
6... d6 {Challenges e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.} *
